      "predicate": "iadas:authorConclusion",
      "object": "Eating attitudes and depressive symptoms were significantly associated. As for attitudes and behaviours related to physical appearance, only EAT scores suggestive of risk were significantly associated with giving up performing to avoid showing the body, feeling very affected comparing the body with others, believing that during performances the audience paid a lot of attention to their body and feeling very affected by it."
    },
    {
      "predicate": "iadas:limites",
      "object": "Our results may be biased by the lack of sincerity among informants or by a failure to report symptoms. The groups includes students of classical ballet and Spanish dance; these two disciplines are not excatly the same: classical ballet tends to underline thinness; this fact may have introduced a bias. We cannot obtain causal conclusions. Therefore we cannot state that particular pressures and school life events trigger risk eating behaviours. The lack of objective data supporting the existence of pressure from coaches."
    },
